{
  "summary": "Decouples block storage from chainstate management across the node and the libbitcoinkernel C API. Callers configure and instantiate `btck_BlockManager` independently and pass it into `btck_chainstate_manager_create`, which holds a shared reference. Block reading functions and block-tree database options are moved from the chainstate manager to the block manager.",
  "problem": "Block storage is instantiated internally inside `ChainstateManager` and tied strictly to its configuration and lifecycle. Kernel callers cannot configure, retain, or manage block storage independently, preventing kernel users from working with pruned block directories.",
  "evidence": [
   "Description notes that 'Pruning (#35627) needs block storage that the caller can configure, keep and prune directly.'",
   "Issue #35627 ('kernel: can't work with pruned blocksdir') documents the limitation in the current kernel architecture."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#36739"
    ],
    "enables": [
      "#35627"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Contains commits from base PR #36739 in its stack. Alters the public libbitcoinkernel C API header `bitcoinkernel.h` and the C++ wrapper `bitcoinkernel_wrapper.h`.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Diff is now available, revealing the removal of `ChainstateLoadOptions::prune` in favor of querying `BlockManager::IsPruneMode` directly, and explicit destruction order management in `NodeContext` and `ChainMan`.",
  "card": "Exposes BlockManager directly in the libbitcoinkernel C API, allowing callers to instantiate, configure, and retain block storage separately from ChainstateManager. Functions for reading blocks and undo data move from the chainstate manager to the block manager handle. This refactoring unblocks pruned block directory support in the kernel (#35627). It depends on #36739.",
  "needs": [],
  "confidence": "high",
  "uncertainties": [],
  "risk": {
    "level": "medium",
    "touches": [
      "validation"
    ],
    "why": "A mistake in block manager ownership, database wiping options, or destruction order could lead to use-after-free crashes or database corruption during node shutdown and kernel reindexing."
  }
}
